page: the browser's side, a reader of nodes

The browser shows, the terminal does, and a page keeps no state; the
shelf only reads, on purpose. A server that speaks the file verbs was
not taken: it needs writes off unless asked, localhost only, and a
header only the shelf sends, for any page you visit can reach localhost.
It makes the page an app. Wait for an app only for meaning across files,
who uses this field or where it is defined, a view apart from the
outline.

The node is the contract, page.html's, frozen; its checker is the oracle
(`medium/page/run.mjs`):
    details#id               id is the address
      summary
        heading              first child: what find and the box list read
        edge ...             the rest: a glyph, a count
      body                   a component: anything with no details#id
Look comes from nesting alone: a node holding nodes is a card, one
holding none a row.

A renderer is a function from one file to nodes; the engine, keys,
folds, find and the address, knows no kind. A kind that needs a parser
gets a generator and a page of its own, as page/py, page/diff and
page/todo do; a kind that is only text stays in the shelf's outline.
Each page stays one file and carries its parts by assembly, as tangle
stitches go, not by a runtime library. devtools takes page.html,
page/diff and page/run.mjs by copy, one way, never edited there.

